This is different from bloom filters and is targetted towards arrays (or files) where 
you dont want to initialize the entire array upfront but do it lazily. 

But if you did that you ll have to intercepts get/set operations to avoid reading junk.

-> My solution is to take a large bitmask that fits into your dcache say 128bits (16bytes)


mask = 0 // uninitialized array
div  = max_array_size / 16

int get(index) {
    pos = index / div;
    if (!checkbit(mask, pos)) 
       return 0; // not initialized yet therefore zero

    return array[index];
}


int set(index, value) {
    pos = index / div;
    if (!checkbit(mask, pos)) {
       setbit(mask, pos);
       localpos = pos * div;
       for (i = 0; i < div; i++) {
       	   array[i + localpos] = 0;
       }
    }
    array[index] = value;
}



